{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nA recurring defect shape — a check whose PASS/absent/empty result is indistinguishable from the\ncheck never having run — appeared nine times in one working session (2026-08-20/21), in two\npopulations. Code review currently has no detection language for it: reviewers confirm an\nassertion is correct without asking the separate question *what else produces this same negative\nresult?* A fail-CLOSED check that misfires is loud and gets fixed; a fail-OPEN check is silent\nand accumulates green history that is then cited as evidence of health. The refinement comment on\nthis ticket adds the mirror: the same broken instrument can also produce a confident FAILING\nresult for a reason other than the condition (the zsh word-splitting false alarm), so the\nlanguage covers \"any definite result\", not just PASS.\n\n**OPERATOR RULING (ticket comment, 2026-08-21): do NOT create a new criterion.** Add the\nfail-open / producer-liveness detection language to the EXISTING code-review criteria: `tests`\n(the test-oracle population) and `error-handling` (the shipped-code population). Escalate back to\na new criterion only if calibration replay proves the language cannot fire reliably from within\nthose two hosts.\n\n### The nine recorded instances (the calibration corpus)\n\nTest-oracle population → hosted by the `tests` criterion:\n\n1. `sound-bumpkinly-thrush` — tests assert a string is ABSENT from a child process's output; a\n   signal-killed child writes nothing, so the string is trivially absent and a run that never\n   executed reports GREEN.\n2. `abusive-pebbly-sakimonkey` — a \"convergence\" test RE-DERIVED the send path's dedup key\n   instead of driving the send path, so it stayed green while live convergence was broken\n   (836 unmarked duplicate Jira comments).\n3. A kill-matrix harness grepped `\"^FAILED\"` against COLORIZED pytest output; it never matched,\n   so it reported \"0 failed\" regardless — all five seeded mutations reported SURVIVING.\n4. A test sweep orphaned in a manual `&` subshell died at ~5%; a truncated log and a clean log\n   produce the SAME empty grep, so \"finished\" and \"killed\" were indistinguishable.\n\nShipped-code population → hosted by the `error-handling` criterion:\n\n5. `obvious-saline-gerbil` — live-DC pass health never checked the child's returncode.\n6. `defiable-distortive-pinniped` — `gen_env_registry` failed OPEN on a helper missing from\n   `KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS`: absent was not an error, it was INVISIBLE (two operator-settable vars\n   undocumented while `--check` stayed green, 2026-08-18→2026-08-21).\n7. `blocky-matricidal-squid` — the `os.environ` half of the same generator, same posture.\n8. A `pgrep -f \"make test\"` monitor MATCHED ITS OWN SHELL, whose command line contained the\n   pattern — the loop waited for itself until timeout regardless of the sweep's outcome.\n9. A `pgrep -f \"review-plan <alias>\"` waiter matched its own process, making a finished\n   operation look like a live hang.\n\n## Approach\n\nDetection language goes where each host criterion's rubric already lives (confirmed by reading\nthe tree):\n\n- The `tests` criterion's full rubric (antipatterns + FP guards) lives in the overlay prompt\n  `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md` (\"This overlay carries the FULL\n  behavioral-testing standard\"). Add a **fail-open oracle** antipattern entry: an assertion on\n  ABSENCE (`not in`, `assert not`, `== \"\"`, empty-collection) derived from a subprocess, file\n  read, network response, or captured output, or a grep/regex over another tool's output used as\n  a pass/fail signal, with NO liveness check on the producer (exit status, non-empty capture, an\n  explicit sentinel). Litmus (the ticket's reviewer prompt, broadened per the refinement\n  comment): *name every world in which this check produces its definite result — pass or fail;\n  if \"the producer never ran / emitted nothing\" is among them, the check cannot distinguish its\n  verdict from its own failure.* Qualify the existing \"greps of COMMAND OUTPUT are valid\" FP\n  guard (item iii) so it applies only when the producer's liveness is established, and add a\n  matching FP guard: an absence assertion PRECEDED by a producer-liveness assertion (returncode\n  check, non-empty-output check, an asserted PRESENCE from the same capture) is NOT flagged.\n- The `error-handling` criterion is a BASE-REVIEWER dimension (criteria_routing.json:\n  \"BASE-REVIEWER dimension (not an overlay in OVERLAY_IDS) — always evaluated by the base\n  reviewer\"), so its detection language goes in\n  `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-base.md` §\"Surface grounded findings\": a short fail-open\n  checks paragraph naming the shipped-code triggers — a lookup/membership MISS path that is\n  silent rather than an error, a subprocess health check that never reads the returncode, a\n  `pgrep`/`pkill` pattern that can match the matching process itself, and any best-effort\n  check whose failure is invisible — with the same one-line litmus and the uniform remediation\n  (assert the producer RAN before trusting any absence derived from it).\n- Record the detection triggers in the criteria registry: extend the `_comment` fields of the\n  `tests` and `error-handling` entries in\n  `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json` (this ticket, the trigger list, and the\n  host-placement ruling). No routing values (posture/threshold/blocking) change.\n- Calibration corpus: add the nine instances to the standing eval specs as recall cases\n  (pre-fix state, `expect: finding`) each paired with its post-fix state (`expect: pass`) —\n  instances 1–4 in `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml`, instances 5–9 in\n  `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-base.eval.yaml` — as minimal seeded diffs in the\n  existing dso-corpus house style.\n- Documentation: code-review criteria have no per-criterion `rebar explain <id>` section (the\n  generated criteria guide + `explain_criterion` cover plan-review criteria only —\n  `src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py` `_explain` / `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_guide.py`);\n  the author-facing code-review surface is the packaged guide printed by `rebar explain review`\n  (`src/rebar/_guides/passing-code-review.md`, per the `docs/passing-code-review.md` pointer).\n  Default taken (recorded as a ticket comment): document the pattern there with two worked\n  examples from the corpus (instance 1, tests; instance 6, error-handling), rather than building\n  a new explain surface for code-review criterion ids — that would be a new capability outside\n  this story's scope and the operator ruling's \"no new criterion (infrastructure)\" spirit.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md` — fail-open oracle antipattern + FP-guard\n  qualification.\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-base.md` — error-handling fail-open detection paragraph.\n- `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json` — `_comment` provenance on the two host\n  entries (no value changes).\n- `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml` — 4 recall + 4 no-fire calibration\n  cases (instances 1–4, pre-fix/post-fix).\n- `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-base.eval.yaml` — 5 recall + 5 no-fire calibration\n  cases (instances 5–9, pre-fix/post-fix).\n- `src/rebar/_guides/passing-code-review.md` — the pattern + two worked examples, served by\n  `rebar explain review`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Offline (no model): `tests/unit/test_code_review_evals.py` validates both extended eval specs\n  strictly (parse, `validate_eval_spec(strict=True)`, registered scorers, balanced fire/no-fire,\n  every case carries a `diff`). Run with the worktree venv:\n  `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_evals.py`.\n- Live calibration replay (bedrock backend, `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE`): run each of the 18 new\n  dataset cases through its Pass-1 prompt via `rebar.llm.evals.eval_solver.run_case`\n  (`code-review-tests` for instances 1–4, `code-review-base` for 5–9); a pre-fix case must\n  produce ≥1 finding, a post-fix case must produce none attributable to the new language.\n  Record per-case results (fire/silent, tagged criteria) as a ticket comment.\n- False-positive posture: the language lands inside criteria that are ALREADY blocking\n  (`tests` @0.54, `error-handling` @0.50 — criteria_routing.json), so there is no advisory\n  ramp to record; instead the FP measurement is (a) the 9 post-fix no-fire cases above and\n  (b) the specs' standing no-fire families (nit-only-style, rule5-structural-grep,\n  four-criterion, boundary-mock, observable-postcondition) staying silent in the same replay.\n  Record both in the calibration comment.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `code-review-tests.md` names the fail-open oracle antipattern (absence-from-captured-\n      output, grep-over-output oracle) with the producer-liveness litmus and a matching FP\n      guard; checked by `rg \"fail-open\" src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md`.\n- [ ] `code-review-base.md` carries the `error-handling` fail-open detection language (silent\n      lookup miss, unchecked returncode, pgrep self-match, invisible best-effort failure);\n      checked by `rg \"fail-open\" src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-base.md`.\n- [ ] The detection triggers are recorded in the criteria registry: the `tests` and\n      `error-handling` entries of `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json` reference\n      this ticket and the trigger list in their `_comment`; no posture/threshold/blocking value\n      changes (checked by diffing the JSON values).\n- [ ] Calibration replay: all 9 pre-fix cases fire (≥1 finding each) and all 9 post-fix cases\n      are silent, verified by the live `run_case` replay; per-case results recorded as a\n      ticket comment.\n- [ ] False-positive posture recorded in the same comment: hosts already blocking, standing\n      no-fire families silent in the replay.\n- [ ] `rebar explain review` documents the pattern with at least two worked examples from the\n      corpus; checked by `rebar explain review | rg -c \"fail-open\"` ≥ 1 and reading the two\n      examples.\n- [ ] Both eval specs stay strict-clean offline; checked by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_evals.py` passing."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787352958907314001,"uuid":"00861765-b5f3-4fab-b55b-ac242d93e2e5"}